#!/usr/bin/python3
"""sl7-ir-lab: one window with the live Surface Laptop 7 IR camera and the
IR emitter test buttons. Run it as your normal user on the Omarchy desktop, on
the "linux-sl7 (IR test)" boot entry:

    sl7-ir-lab

The window shows the camera live the whole time: raw GREY 644x604 frames read
from the sl7-ir-bridge loopback (/dev/v4l/by-id/sl7-ir-camera) through
`v4l2-ctl --stream-mmap --stream-to=-`, reopened whenever the bridge ends its
10 s session. Under the image: the mean and maximum of every frame and a 10 s
graph of the mean with a marker for each pulse.

Pulse 12.5 / 25 / 50 mA ask "Fire IR pulse at NN mA per channel?" and then run

    pkexec /usr/bin/sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --led ir \
        max_ma_per_channel=NN --pulse-delay 2 --confirmed

without blocking the window (the camera keeps streaming). --confirmed replaces
the typed phrase for 12.5, 25 and 50 mA per channel only; the approval file, the
IR test boot, the per-boot pulse limit, the hardware timer and the ir_max_ua cap
all still apply, and there is no 100 mA button. The emitter test sets the IR
sensor to manual maximum exposure itself and restores it on every exit path.
Stage C section (linux-sl7 7.2.8-21 and omarchy-surface-sl7 39 or later): buttons C0a, C0b,
C1 (SW), C1 (HW) and T0b run `pkexec sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --stage NAME
--confirmed` after a Fire / Cancel dialog. Each button is enabled only when the stage before
it is recorded OK (read with `pkexec sl7-ir-emitter-test --status`, press Status). The stage
tool starts the IR sensor itself with led_mode set before the stream starts, so the lab
pauses its own camera reader for the run (the live view freezes, the graph pauses, a banner
says so) and reconnects when the tool ends; the result (OK or FAIL, STATUS bits decoded:
"CH1 open", "ramp-up done", "timer expired") and the stage's frame means with the pulse or
arm times are shown in the window afterwards.

After the pulse the lab looks for a spike in the frames around the fire time
(median and robust sd, as the old analyser did), shows the brightest frame next
to a baseline frame for a few seconds in the window, and keeps the tool output
(REGDUMP lines, kernel strobe snapshot) in the collapsible panel. Everything is
saved under ~/sl7-ir-lab/<timestamp>/.
"""
